    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term in the expansion of \\((x+y)^6\\), we apply the Binomial Theorem. The Binomial Theorem states that\n\n\\[\n(a + b)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} a^{n-k} b^k.\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, \\(a = x\\), \\(b = y\\), and \\(n = 6\\). We are looking for the term where the powers of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are 2 and 4, respectively. This corresponds to the term where \\(k = 4\\) in the expansion. The general term in the expansion is given by\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{k} x^{6-k} y^k.\n\\]\n\nFor the term involving \\(x^2y^4\\), we set \\(k = 4\\). Substituting \\(k = 4\\) into the general term, we get\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} x^{6-4} y^4 = \\binom{6}{4} x^2 y^4.\n\\]\n\nThe coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term is \\(\\binom{6}{4}\\). We calculate \\(\\binom{6}{4}\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} = \\frac{6!}{4!2!} = \\frac{6 \\times 5 \\times 4!}{4! \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{6 \\times 5}{2 \\times 1} = 15.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term in the expansion of \\((x+y)^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
